tools/check_isa_level.py-3-
tools/check_isa_level.py-4-Each disassembled instruction mnemonic is mapped back to the official ARM ISA
tools/check_isa_level.py-5-catalog (`experiments/m7-isa-coverage/isa-catalog.json`) and its feature
tools/check_isa_level.py-6-expressions are evaluated against a target feature level. An instruction is a
tools/check_isa_level.py-7-violation when every catalog encoding requires a feature level above the
tools/check_isa_level.py-8-target, e.g. any SVE2-only opcode in a Kunpeng 920B (FEAT_SVE, VL=256)
tools/check_isa_level.py-9-object.
tools/check_isa_level.py-10-
tools/check_isa_level.py:11:Feature levels (rank order):
tools/check_isa_level.py-12-    sve1 < sve2 (=sve_bitperm) < sve2p1 < sve2p2 < sve2p3 < sme
tools/check_isa_level.py-13-
tools/check_isa_level.py-14-Limitations (documented, not silent):
tools/check_isa_level.py-15-  - Multi-register SVE table lookup (TBL2) is covered by an operand-pattern
tools/check_isa_level.py-16-    rule (`tbl`/`tbx` with a `{zN.x-zM.x}` two-register table) because the
tools/check_isa_level.py-17-    catalog's `asm` string only spells TBL and objdump prints `tbl` for both
tools/check_isa_level.py-18-    the SVE1 one-register and SVE2 two-register encodings.
tools/check_isa_level.py-19-  - A mnemonic whose encodings span levels (e.g. TBL: one SVE1, one SVE2
